# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Kuyash Farms — frontend environment
#
# Copy to `.env.local` for development. Values are read at BUILD time: Next.js
# inlines every NEXT_PUBLIC_* variable into the bundle, so changing one in a
# running container has no effect — you must rebuild.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Base URL of the Django API, including the version prefix and no trailing slash.
# Required in production: the build fails loudly rather than silently defaulting
# to localhost and breaking in front of a customer.
#
# **The browser resolves this, not the server** — so it must name a host the
# browser can reach. Under WSL2 the server is in the VM and the browser is on
# Windows, so `localhost` depends on WSL's localhost forwarding being healthy.
# When it is not, use the VM's address (`hostname -I`) here and start the
# backend with `make run`, which binds 0.0.0.0. See .env.local for the trade-off
# against the SameSite refresh cookie.
#
#   local   http://localhost:8000/api/v1
#   prod    https://api.kuyashfarms.com/api/v1
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8000/api/v1

# The site's own public URL, used to build absolute metadata URLs.
#
# Open Graph and Twitter images MUST be absolute: Facebook, WhatsApp and
# Twitter fetch them from their own servers and have no idea what your origin
# is, so a relative URL is no image at all. Next resolves them against this
# value via `metadataBase`.
#
# Getting it wrong fails silently and only off-site: the page renders
# perfectly, and the share card either does not appear or points at the wrong
# domain. Nothing in the build or in local testing will tell you.
#
#   local   http://localhost:3000
#   prod    https://kuyashfarms.com
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing secret belongs in this file.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* variables are shipped to every visitor's browser. Never put a
# Supabase service-role key, a database URL, a Paystack SECRET key or an SMTP
# credential here — they belong in the backend's own .env. The Paystack public
# key is not needed either: the browser is redirected to Paystack's hosted
# checkout and never initialises the SDK itself.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Build-time escape hatch for CI. **Never set this in a real deployment.**
#
# `/categories` and the academy pages prerender against the API, so a build with
# no backend reachable fails with ECONNREFUSED. CI has no backend, so it sets
# this to let *only* fetches that supplied an `offlineFallback` render empty
# instead. In production an unreachable API should stop the release rather than
# quietly ship a shop with no products in it.
# NEXT_PRERENDER_OFFLINE=

# Google sign-in (PRD §13 Q13) — optional.
#
# Public by design: it appears in the redirect URL every visitor is sent to, so
# inlining it is correct here in a way it never would be for a secret. The
# client *secret* lives only on the backend.
#
# Leave blank and the Google button renders nothing — a deployment without a
# Google project should show no button rather than one that fails when pressed.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
